{"id":"returns-changed","title":"Returns that were amended, or that changed the rules they are kept by","question":"Which organizations filed an amended return that moved their figures, switched accounting method, or changed their financial year, and did the change leave months that no return covers?","finding":"75 tax years have more than one version of the return on file; in 32 the amendment moved revenue, expenses or year-end net assets by $25,000 or more (and 1% of expenses), most of all at Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc for the year ended 30 June 2023, where year-end net assets went from $20.0 million to $40.5 million. 19 returns report a different accounting method from the return before. 19 returns follow a change of financial year, and in 3 of those cases months are left that no return on file covers.","how":"Every version of every return in the IRS files is kept; versions of the same tax year are ordered by filing timestamp and the first is compared with the last. The accounting method is the box ticked on each return (cash, accrual or other), compared with the return before. A change of financial year is a year-end month different from the return before, or a return shorter than eleven months that is neither a first nor a final return; the dates are then checked for months that fall between two returns and are covered by no tax period in the IRS index or in ProPublica's list. Ordered: amended returns by size of change, then method changes and uncovered months, then other year changes.","limits":" An organization may change its year-end, for instance to match its funders' fiscal year; the change normally shows as one short return. Switching back and forth between cash and accrual accounting often points to a change of preparer. What each does is break the series: the years on either side cannot be compared without care, and months with no return are months nobody outside can see. A short return may exist and simply be missing from the public files. City totals are from the Controller's table of every payment, less loans, district assessments handed over, benefit-plan trust payments and debt service, and include aid that passes through an organization to others. A pattern here is a reason to ask for the audited statements, not a finding.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"pattern","t":"What changed","type":"text"},{"k":"year","t":"Tax year ended","type":"date"},{"k":"detail","t":"Detail","type":"text"},{"k":"revBefore","t":"Revenue, first version","type":"usd"},{"k":"revAfter","t":"Revenue, latest version","type":"usd"},{"k":"expChange","t":"Change in expenses","type":"usd"},{"k":"naBefore","t":"Net assets, first version","type":"usd"},{"k":"naAfter","t":"Net assets, latest version","type":"usd"},{"k":"cityPaid","t":"City paid in that tax year","type":"usd"},{"k":"ret","t":"Source","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"org":"Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc","orgKey":"EPISCOPAL COMMUNITY SERVICES SAN FRANCISCO","year":"2023-06-30","ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/943096716/202542039349301659/full"},"pattern":"Amended return changed the figures","detail":"First filed 15 May 2024; latest version filed 22 July 2025.","revBefore":61587026,"revAfter":61588691,"revChange":1665,"expChange":1830,"naBefore":19950972,"naAfter":40470355,"naChange":20519383,"cityPaid":51626701},{"org":"Illuminate The Arts","orgKey":"ILLUMINATE ARTS","year":"2024-06-30","ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/453717224/202641329349307034/full"},"pattern":"Amended return changed the figures","detail":"First filed 15 May 2025; latest version filed 12 May 2026.","revBefore":12990069,"revAfter":9623584,"revChange":-3366485,"expChange":0,"naBefore":10707454,"naAfter":8300969,"naChange":-2406485,"cityPaid":262510},{"org":"Springboard Collaborative","orgKey":"SPRINGBOARD COLLABORATIVE","year":"2023-12-31","ret":{"t":"Return","u"